nodecode-steward is a rewrite of Sahil-SS9's Sahil-SS9/hermes-project-stewardship:

  https://github.com/Sahil-SS9/hermes-project-stewardship
  commit cc54a15ccb841c69b4e898ce91c98e6f5ee23444
  Apache License 2.0, Copyright (c) 2026 Sahil Saghir (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the plugin's core workflow, natively in Common Lisp. A
project is a record with an autonomy level on the same six-rung ladder
(assistant, investigator, planner, builder, maintainer, steward) whose
capabilities only ever widen; its health is measured by running the
project's own declared checks, through an executable allowlist with no shell,
in the project's own directory, and graded by the same rule (healthy, watch,
degraded, unknown when nothing could be measured); an initiative may be
proposed only at planner or above and only on a health that is neither
unknown nor critical, and a person approves or rejects it; and the
pre_llm_call hook that gave a bound bot its project's memory is a :ROUND
hook that puts a bound session's project line in the request. The registry
is one JSON file under the Nodecode home instead of SQLite.

What was left out, and why: the plugin's Python package, its SQLite schema
and migrations, its localhost dashboard and desktop panel, its Kanban board
bridge (which reads another application's private modules), its notification
and gateway stack, the objective/goal/milestone ladder and its GitHub `gh`
evidence, and its CLI (`stewardctl`). None of them has a native counterpart
in a cell, and the workflow above is what a session actually uses. The
per-project memory is kept as one briefing text a person writes, rather than
the original's rules/decisions/facts tables.
